 # Working notes: 1e26763a6f70
 
-Record design decisions, hypotheses, review findings, and unresolved questions here.
-Imported associated notes retain original evidence/timestamps in `evidence/associated_notes.jsonl`.
+## Authority and intended rule
+
+The unchanged selected Racket authority is pinned by `RULE_HASH = "dffe1ada0168"`. For every nonzero color that appears exactly twice, the authority fills the empty integer midpoint when the endpoints share a row or column and their positive span is even. The generator produces inputs only; Racket remains the label authority.
+
+## Repaired generator contract
+
+Version 2.0.0 advertises only three observable axes: `grid_h` 5..12, `grid_w` 6..14, and `pairs` 1..5. Exact overrides are strict in every difficulty band; unknown keys, bools/non-integers, negative `sample_index`, and unknown difficulty names are rejected. A backtracking placer constructs exactly the requested number of endpoint/midpoint triples, including the tight 5x6, five-pair boundary, rather than silently underplacing after a rejection-loop cap.
+
+Each generated color is distinct and occurs at exactly two endpoints. Endpoint and midpoint cells are disjoint across pairs. Pair 1 is always span 2; its orientation alternates by `sample_index`. When present, pair 2 is span 4 in the opposite orientation. Remaining pairs use any lawful even span. This gives both orientations and deliberately refutes a span-four-only shortcut while preserving ordinary variability.
+
+Every third `sample_index` is a canonical-profile anchor. Unless explicitly overridden, it selects one of the exact canonical input shapes `(5,7)`, `(7,6)`, or `(7,7)` with one pair. The first design anchored every fifth sample; the profile-floor gate found only 4/20 exact canonical profiles in one band, so the schedule was changed to every third sample and rechecked at >=6/20 in all bands.
+
+`self_check` now validates rectangular ARC-color grids, exactly two cells per active color, row/column alignment, positive even span, and an initially empty midpoint. `strict_canonical_like` additionally checks generated-domain dimensions and pair count. `observe_overrides` recovers all three advertised controls from pixels.
+
+## Shortcut evidence
+
+All canonical demonstrations use span 4, so the strongest compact rival is `span_four_only`: perform the midpoint fill only for span-4 pairs. The lawful 5x6 witness in `counterexamples/span_four_only.json` has color 2 at `(2,1)` and `(2,3)`; the intended output also colors `(2,2)`, while the rival leaves it zero. `SEMANTIC_RIVALS`, `CANONICAL_SHORTCUTS`, `LESSON_COMPETITORS`, and `WITNESS_RIVALS` bind this rival to the `non_four_span` witness. Every generated grid contains a non-four span.
+
+## Retired behavior
+
+The prior implementation had no rule pin, declared dead texture/palette controls, routed ordinary sampling through declared negative textures, accepted semantically invalid odd/diagonal/obstructed pairs in `self_check`, silently coerced or ignored invalid overrides, and could return fewer pairs than requested. Those behaviors were removed rather than preserved as compatibility surface. The canonical Racket row was not edited.
+
+## Known limits
+
+The semantic evidence is finite and names one high-value rival; it does not prove identifiability against every possible hypothesis. The generated domain intentionally caps at five pairs and at even spans fitting the 5..12 by 6..14 canvas. Backtracking protects only endpoint/midpoint disjointness; other empty cells may lie between endpoints, which is lawful under the selected rule. Canonical-profile anchoring covers the observed shape/pair-count profile, not every possible spatial arrangement or color. Associated retained notes were empty, so no earlier design rationale was available beyond the supplied baseline and current authority.
